# Lab 1 — KMS Envelope Encryption + CloudTrail Audit Logging for PHI

## Scenario
[2–3 sentences. The hospital storing lab results in S3. What HIPAA
control this maps to — §164.312(a)(2)(iv) encryption + §164.312(b)
audit controls. Not a story here — that lives in the writeup.]

## What gets built
- Customer-Managed KMS key with rotation enabled and a least-privilege key policy
- S3 bucket with default SSE-KMS encryption using the CMK
- CloudTrail trail capturing management events + KMS data events + S3 data events
- Dedicated S3 bucket for CloudTrail logs, encrypted with a separate CMK
- Two IAM test principals: one authorized to decrypt, one explicitly denied

## What gets demonstrated
- Every PHI object in S3 is encrypted at rest with a customer-controlled key
- Every key-use event (Encrypt, Decrypt, GenerateDataKey) is logged with caller identity, timestamp, and source IP
- An unauthorized decrypt attempt is denied AND logged — both halves matter
- The audit trail is queryable via Athena

